sec.p:
In practice, it may be quite difficult to calculate $\mathbb {P}_{0}(\delta _{\gamma _{0},\gamma _{1}}=1)$ and $\mathbb {P}_{1}(\delta _{\gamma _{0},\gamma _{1}}=0)$ exactly [1], [5]. Therefore, we cannot choose $\gamma _{0}$ and $\gamma _{1}$ such that the type I error is equal to a desired level $\alpha \in (0,1)$ and the type II error is equal to a desired level $\beta \in (0,1)$. For this reason, Wand [1], [2] introduced approximations for $\gamma _{1}$ and $\gamma _{0}$:
\begin{equation*}\gamma _{1}=\frac {1-\beta }{\alpha } \text {and}~\gamma _{0}=\frac {\beta }{1-\alpha }. \tag {4}\end{equation*}Wand [1] showed that at least one of the two errors will be controlled at the desired level:
\begin{equation*} \mathbb {P}_{0}(\delta _{\gamma _{0},\gamma _{1}}=1)+\mathbb {P}_{1}(\delta _{\gamma _{0},\gamma _{1}}=0)\leq \alpha + \beta . \tag {5}\end{equation*}
